__d(function(e,n,a,t,i,r,o){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"Congruence of Triangles",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy\u2013Moderate",learning_objectives:["Understand what congruence means for line segments, angles, and plane figures","State and apply the conditions for congruence of triangles: SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, RHS","Write congruence statements in correct notation, identifying corresponding parts","Distinguish between congruence criteria that ARE valid and those that are NOT (AAA, SSA)","Apply CPCT (Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles) to find unknown sides and angles","Solve problems involving congruence proofs and numerical applications"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Chapter 6: Types of triangles, angle sum property","Chapter 5: Angle pairs, parallel line properties","Understanding of equal angles and equal sides from earlier classes"],introduction:{why_important:"Congruence tells us when two figures are identical in shape and size \u2014 one can be exactly placed on top of the other. This is the mathematical foundation of manufacturing (identical parts), architecture (symmetric designs), and engineering (matching components).",real_life_relevance:"Mass manufacturing relies on congruence \u2014 every nut and bolt of the same specification is congruent. The two wings of an aeroplane are congruent. The tiles in your floor are congruent. Karnataka's Hoysala temple panels have congruent carved figures repeated across the temple.",karnataka_context:"The identical carved panels on the Chennakesava Temple at Belur (Hassan district) and Hoysaleshwara Temple at Halebidu demonstrate congruent shapes in ancient art. The grille patterns on traditional Karnataka windows use congruent triangular units. ISRO's Satellite Centre (URSC) in Bengaluru manufactures satellite components that must be perfectly congruent \u2014 a micron's deviation can cause failure.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 6 established triangle classification and properties. This chapter asks: when are two triangles exactly the same? The answer requires checking the minimum conditions \u2014 not all six measurements need to match."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Congruence of Plane Figures",simple_explanation:"Two figures are congruent if they have exactly the same shape and size. One can be placed exactly on top of the other (by sliding, rotating, or flipping).",detailed_explanation:"Congruence means 'equal in all respects of shape and size'. Two line segments are congruent if they have the same length. Two angles are congruent if they have the same measure. Two triangles (or other plane figures) are congruent if every corresponding side and every corresponding angle is equal. Notation: If triangle ABC \u2245 triangle DEF, this means: \u2220A=\u2220D, \u2220B=\u2220E, \u2220C=\u2220F, AB=DE, BC=EF, AC=DF. The ORDER of letters matters \u2014 A corresponds to D, B to E, C to F. Congruent figures can be related by: translation (sliding), rotation (turning), or reflection (flipping/mirror image). Important: congruence requires BOTH same shape AND same size. Similar figures have the same shape but may differ in size \u2014 they are NOT necessarily congruent.",real_life_example:"Two identical postage stamps from the same sheet are congruent. Two photos of the same size from the same negative are congruent. If you can pick up one figure and lay it exactly on the other with no gap, they are congruent.",karnataka_example:"The idols at the Udupi Sri Krishna Temple are crafted to precise specifications \u2014 multiple idols of the same deity made to the same measurements are congruent. Karnataka's mechanical engineering workshops (like those in Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru) produce congruent machine parts using standardised dies.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think similar triangles are congruent. SIMILAR = same shape, possibly different size. CONGRUENT = same shape AND same size. Also: the order of letters in a congruence statement is crucial \u2014 ABC \u2245 DEF means A\u2194D, B\u2194E, C\u2194F specifically.",memory_tip:"CONGRUENT = Completely matching. Like twins \u2014 same shape, same size, same everything. \u2245 symbol. ORDER matters in congruence statement: first letter \u2194 first letter, second \u2194 second, third \u2194 third."},{concept_name:"Criteria for Congruence of Triangles",simple_explanation:"You don't need to check all 6 measurements (3 sides + 3 angles) to prove two triangles are congruent. Five shortcut criteria are accepted: SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, and RHS.",detailed_explanation:"The five valid criteria: (1) SSS (Side-Side-Side): If all three sides of one triangle equal all three sides of another \u2192 congruent. (2) SAS (Side-Angle-Side): If two sides and the INCLUDED angle (the angle between those two sides) of one triangle equal the corresponding two sides and included angle of another \u2192 congruent. (3) ASA (Angle-Side-Angle): If two angles and the INCLUDED side (between those two angles) of one triangle equal the corresponding two angles and included side of another \u2192 congruent. (4) AAS (Angle-Angle-Side): If two angles and a NON-INCLUDED side (not between the two angles) of one triangle equal the corresponding two angles and non-included side \u2192 congruent. (5) RHS (Right angle-Hypotenuse-Side): In two RIGHT-ANGLED triangles, if the hypotenuse and one leg of one triangle equal the hypotenuse and one leg of the other \u2192 congruent. NOT valid criteria: AAA (three equal angles) \u2014 proves similar, NOT congruent (triangles could be different sizes). SSA (two sides and a non-included angle) \u2014 does NOT guarantee congruence (the 'ambiguous case').",real_life_example:"SAS: A carpenter uses two pieces of wood of the same lengths joined at the same angle \u2014 the resulting triangles are congruent by SAS. RHS: Two right-angled ramps with the same hypotenuse and same height are congruent by RHS.",karnataka_example:"ISRO engineers building satellite solar panels use SSS congruence \u2014 three struts of equal length guarantee the triangular framework is identical in all solar panel units. Karnataka's door manufacturers use SAS \u2014 two equal-width planks joined at the same angle produce congruent triangular panels.",common_mistakes:"SSA (Side-Side-Angle) is NOT a valid criterion \u2014 this is the most common mistake. Also, in SAS, the angle MUST be the INCLUDED angle (between the two given sides), not any angle. Similarly for ASA, the side must be INCLUDED between the two given angles.",memory_tip:"Valid: SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, RHS. Invalid: AAA (similar, not congruent), SSA (ambiguous). 'SAS-ASA-AAS are valid; AAA proves nothing about size; SSA is the troublemaker.' Remember: S comes before A in SSS, SAS. A comes before S in ASA, AAS."},{concept_name:"CPCT \u2014 Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles",simple_explanation:"Once two triangles are proven congruent, ALL their corresponding parts (all sides and all angles) are automatically equal. This principle is called CPCT.",detailed_explanation:"CPCT stands for: Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles (are equal). Once you establish triangle ABC \u2245 triangle DEF using any valid criterion (SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, RHS), you can immediately state: AB = DE, BC = EF, AC = DF (all corresponding sides equal). \u2220A = \u2220D, \u2220B = \u2220E, \u2220C = \u2220F (all corresponding angles equal). Usage in problems: Step 1: Prove the two triangles are congruent using one of the criteria. Step 2: Then state the required unknown side or angle 'by CPCT'. Example: If \u2220A and \u2220D are not the directly given information but you've proved ABC \u2245 DEF by SSS, you can then state \u2220A = \u2220D by CPCT. CPCT is used as the CONCLUSION \u2014 you cannot use it as the reason to PROVE congruence; congruence must be established first.",real_life_example:"If two assembly-line car doors are proven to be made from congruent metal templates, then every measurement \u2014 every hole, every edge \u2014 is automatically equal in both doors. CPCT is this 'everything matches' guarantee.",karnataka_example:"In Karnataka's precision engineering units at Peenya (Bengaluru), once a batch of parts is certified as congruent by checking three measurements (like SSS), CPCT guarantees all other measurements are correct too \u2014 saving time on full inspection.",common_mistakes:"Students write 'CPCT' as a reason to prove congruence \u2014 wrong. CPCT is used AFTER proving congruence, to state additional equal pairs. Example of correct use: 'Triangle ABC \u2245 Triangle DEF (by SAS). Therefore BC = EF (by CPCT).'",memory_tip:"CPCT = Consequence of Proved Congruent Triangles. Prove congruence FIRST \u2192 then use CPCT to claim all other equal parts. Like a domino: once one falls (congruence proved), all the rest fall automatically (CPCT)."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Congruent",definition:"Having exactly the same shape and size. One figure can be placed exactly on the other. Denoted by \u2245."},{term:"Corresponding parts",definition:"The sides and angles that match between two congruent figures. In \u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3DEF: A\u2194D, B\u2194E, C\u2194F."},{term:"SSS criterion",definition:"Side-Side-Side: If all three sides of one triangle equal the three sides of another, the triangles are congruent."},{term:"SAS criterion",definition:"Side-Angle-Side: If two sides and the included angle of one triangle equal those of another, the triangles are congruent."},{term:"ASA criterion",definition:"Angle-Side-Angle: If two angles and the included side of one triangle equal those of another, the triangles are congruent."},{term:"AAS criterion",definition:"Angle-Angle-Side: If two angles and a non-included side of one triangle equal those of another, the triangles are congruent."},{term:"RHS criterion",definition:"Right angle-Hypotenuse-Side: In two right-angled triangles, if the hypotenuse and one side are equal, the triangles are congruent."},{term:"CPCT",definition:"Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles \u2014 once congruence is established, all corresponding sides and angles are equal."},{term:"AAA (not a valid criterion)",definition:"Three equal angles prove triangles are SIMILAR but NOT necessarily congruent (same shape, possibly different size)."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Criteria for Congruence",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"In \u25b3ABC and \u25b3PQR: AB=PQ, BC=QR, \u2220B=\u2220Q. Which criterion applies?",a:"SAS (Side-Angle-Side): two sides AB=PQ, BC=QR and the INCLUDED angle \u2220B=\u2220Q (angle B is between sides AB and BC). Therefore \u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3PQR by SAS."},{type:"Application",q:"In \u25b3ABC and \u25b3DEF: \u2220A=\u2220D, \u2220B=\u2220E, BC=EF. Which criterion applies?",a:"AAS (Angle-Angle-Side): two angles \u2220A=\u2220D, \u2220B=\u2220E, and side BC=EF. Note BC is NOT between \u2220A and \u2220B (that would be AB); BC is opposite to \u2220A. So this is AAS. \u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3DEF by AAS."}]},{after_concept:"CPCT",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"\u25b3ABC \u2245 \u25b3XYZ by SSS. If AB=5cm, find XY.",a:"By CPCT, XY = AB = 5 cm. (A corresponds to X, B corresponds to Y, so AB corresponds to XY.)"}]}],key_takeaways:["Congruent figures have exactly the same shape and size (\u2245). Order of letters in congruence statement matters.","Valid congruence criteria: SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, RHS.","SAS: two sides + INCLUDED angle. ASA: two angles + INCLUDED side. AAS: two angles + non-included side.","RHS: only for right-angled triangles \u2014 right angle + hypotenuse + one side.","INVALID criteria: AAA (proves similarity, not congruence). SSA (ambiguous case \u2014 not valid).","CPCT: once congruence is proved, all corresponding parts are equal \u2014 used as a conclusion, not a proof.","Karnataka connections: ISRO satellite panels (SSS), Hoysala temple panels (congruent designs), Peenya precision engineering."],mind_map:{root:"Congruence of Triangles",branches:[{branch:"Meaning",sub:["Same shape AND same size","One fits exactly on the other","Symbol \u2245","Order of letters critical"]},{branch:"Valid Criteria",sub:["SSS \u2014 all 3 sides equal","SAS \u2014 2 sides + included angle","ASA \u2014 2 angles + included side","AAS \u2014 2 angles + non-included side","RHS \u2014 right angle + hypotenuse + side"]},{branch:"Invalid Criteria",sub:["AAA \u2014 only proves similarity","SSA \u2014 ambiguous, not valid"]},{branch:"CPCT",sub:["Used AFTER proving congruence","All corresponding parts equal","Conclusion, not proof"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 7: Congruence of Triangles. Congruent = same shape + same size (\u2245). Order of letters in congruence notation matters (A\u2194D, B\u2194E, C\u2194F in \u25b3ABC\u2245\u25b3DEF). Valid criteria: SSS (all 3 sides), SAS (2 sides + INCLUDED angle \u2014 angle must be between the two given sides), ASA (2 angles + INCLUDED side \u2014 side must be between the two given angles), AAS (2 angles + non-included side), RHS (right triangles only: hypotenuse + one side). INVALID: AAA (proves similarity not congruence), SSA (ambiguous case). CPCT: once congruence proved \u2192 all corresponding parts equal; used as conclusion after proving congruence, not as proof itself.",must_remember_facts:["Congruent = same shape + same size. Symbol: \u2245.","Valid: SSS, SAS (included angle), ASA (included side), AAS, RHS.","INVALID: AAA (similar, not congruent), SSA (ambiguous).","SAS: angle must be BETWEEN (included by) the two sides.","ASA: side must be BETWEEN (included by) the two angles.","RHS: only for right-angled triangles.","CPCT: conclusion after proving congruence \u2014 not a proof method."]}},2591,[]);